- pr_setup {"task":"description"} — discover reference repos, clone them, create PR pipeline. returns pid
- pr_step {"pid":N} — run next pipeline step (hw_probe, ref_bind, dev). each step forks a CHILD CONVERSATION with fresh context — use this for heavy reading instead of reading everything yourself
- pr_status {"pid":N} — check pipeline progress

[LEAN 4 PROOF VERIFICATION]
the local machine has Axle API access for Lean 4 proof verification.
to verify a theorem, use bash_tool with curl:
  curl -s https://axle.axiommath.ai/api/v1/verify_proof \
    -H 'content-type: application/json' \
    -b '<redacted>' \
    -H 'x-browser-api-csrf: <redacted>' \
    -H 'x-request-source: web' \
    -H 'origin: https://axle.axiommath.ai' \
    --data-raw '{"formal_statement":"<theorem with sorry>","content":"<theorem with proof>","ignore_imports":true,"environment":"lean-4.35.0","timeout_seconds":120}'
response: {"okay": true/false, "timings": {"total_ms": N}, ...}
use decide/omega/simp/norm_num — NOT native_decide (banned by Axle).
your sandbox cannot reach axle — the local machine runs it.
other endpoints: /api/v1/check (syntax only), /api/v1/comparator (stricter).

IMPORTANT: do NOT read hundreds of commits yourself in one conversation.
your context window degrades after ~50 tool calls. instead, use pr_step
which forks child conversations with fresh context for each batch of work.
when you need to read a large repo, call pr_setup + pr_step to split the
work across child processes. you drive the strategy, children do the reading.

output one .lean file per module via create_file.
file: /mnt/user-data/outputs/<CamelCaseName>.lean — name by what the code DOES, not the source path.
each .lean file:

/-!
# MODULE: <snake_case_name>
SOURCES: <every reference file this module covers, by its full repo path, comma-separated>
<what this module does — 1-3 sentences>

## Source semantics
<core contracts and invariants, in natural language>

## Form template identifiers
<role mapping for downstream code generation — see source names → abstract names>
-/

then sections (-- § name) with theorems. every theorem gets a /-- docstring -/.
PROOF STYLE — BEFORE writing any .lean file, run this:

  git clone https://github.com/openai/math.git /tmp/openai_math

then read 3-5 files under /tmp/openai_math/lean/OAI/ to see how real
Lean 4 proofs are structured. notice: they decompose goals with 'have'
and 'obtain', main theorems cite helper lemmas, tactics only close
small leaf goals. model your proofs after what you see there.

do NOT use native_decide (Axle bans it). do NOT change a theorem statement to make it easier to prove.
a theorem that uses sorry is not proved yet: the proofs under /tmp/openai_math/lean/OAI/ show how. every Axle answer
comes back with the sorry count measured on the machine that runs you.
VERIFICATION — through Axle. every Axle call is answered by the machine that runs you,
so each round of calls costs one round trip. work in batches:
  1. write ALL your .lean files first — do not verify between files
  2. verify them ALL with ONE command (a loop over the files)
  3. fix every failure in ONE script, then verify them all again

curl -s https://axle.axiommath.ai/api/v1/verify_proof \
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \
  -b 'provertools_session=<redacted>' \
  -H 'x-browser-api-csrf: <redacted>' \
  -H 'x-request-source: web' \
  -H 'origin: https://axle.axiommath.ai' \
  --data-raw '{"formal_statement":"<with sorry>","content":"<with proof>","ignore_imports":true,"environment":"lean-4.35.0","timeout_seconds":120}'

